Bridging the divide between 2D inorganic and organic materials is a recent class of “bottom-up” compounds assembled from multitopic dithiolene and $o$ -semiquinone aromatic organic moieties bridged by square-planar metal ions. These 2D metal−organic networks exhibit non-zero bandgaps and good electrical conductivity, enabled by full charge delocalization in the 2D plane.19−21 They can therefore be described as semiconducting metal−organic graphene analogues (s-MOGs). Certain members of this class have also recently been predicted to behave as topological insulators,22,23 a realm currently dominated by purely inorganic compounds.24 Clearly, the synthesis and characterization of new $s$ -MOGs could give rise to important new electronic materials with exotic electronic states and potential applications in the semiconductor device industry.
Inspired by the success of dithiolene-based $s$ -MOGs, whose metal linkages mimic classic Class III-delocalized homoleptic Ni(dithiolene)2 complexes,25,26 we identified $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{sq})_{2}$ $\left(\operatorname{isq}=o\right.$ - diiminobenzosemiquinonate) as an attractive target for the construction of a fully charge-delocalized $s{\mathrm{.}}$ -MOG. Although first isolated in $1927^{27^{4}}$ from $o$ -phenylenediamine and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ in ammoniacal water, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{q}})_{2}$ evaded structural and electronic characterization for quite a long time28,29 but is now known to be fully $\pi$ -conjugated, with its ground state having partial singlet biradical character.30,31 Crystalline $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{q}})_{2}$ itself exhibits high mobility in organic field effect transistors32 and increased conductivity upon doping with $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ .33 Here, we show that twodimensional extension of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{sq})_{2}$ through the reaction of 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenylene hexahydrochloride (HATP·6HCl) with ammoniacal $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ produces a new crystalline $s$ -MOG with very high electrical conductivity that is linearly proportional to temperature. Remarkably, the conductivity of the new material vastly exceeds that of previous $s$ -MOGs and other conductive MOFs and is higher than even some of the best organic conductors.
Under conditions mimicking the synthesis of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{isq})_{2},$ HATP·6HCl was treated with an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ · $\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under air, followed by the addition of aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ under constant stirring. The reaction mixture was heated to $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stirred under air for an additional $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This yielded the new material $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{HITP}~=~2,3,6,7,10,1\right]$ -hexaiminotriphenylenesemiquinonate) as a bulk black powder and a very dark blue-violet film (see Scheme 1). Upon extensive washing with water in an ultrasonic bath, the charge neutrality of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{\bar{HITP}})_{2},$ and implicitly the formation of monoanionic $o$ - aOnly the closed-shell resonance structure is shown, although diradical Ni-bisdiimine linkages, shown on the bottom and found in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{isq})_{2},$ are likely here too.
diiminobenzosemiquinonate moieties (Scheme 1, bottom), was confirmed by elemental analysis and $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Figures S1 and S2, XPS spectra of both powder and films (vide infra) of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ show the presence of only Ni, N, C, and $\mathrm{~O~}$ resonance peaks from the material itself and water guest molecules. Cl peaks, expected for any trapped or charge-balancing anionic chlorides that would compensate a possible cationic structure, are absent. Similarly, high-resolution analysis of the $\mathrm{Ni}(2\mathrm{p})$ and $\mathrm{{N}}(1\mathrm{{s}})$ regions of the XPS spectra show a single type of ${\bf N i}$ and $\mathbf{N}$ atoms, respectively, suggesting that no extraneous $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ ions are present. These ions are the only possible cationic species that could balance an anionic material, and they indicate that $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is indeed neutral, as expected from its similarity with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{q}})_{2}$ .
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ revealed a crystalline structure with prominent peaks at $2\theta=$ $4.7^{\circ},9.5^{\circ},12.6^{\circ}.$ , and $16.5^{\circ}\mathrm{~.~}$ , indicative of long-range order within the $a b$ plane (see Figure 1). An additional, weaker and broader peak at $2\theta\:=\:27.3^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the [001] reflections, is indicative of poorer long-rage order along the $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{c}}}$ direction, as expected for covalently linked layered materials. We simulated several possible stacking arrangements for the 2D sheets of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP}\bar{)_{2}},$ including staggered $\left({\mathrm{AA}}^{-1}\right)$ , eclipsed (AA), and slipped-parallel (AB) orientations. As shown in Figure 1, the experimental pattern rules out the $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}$ stacking but agrees very well with both the AA and AB sequences. Although the AA and AB sequences are impossible to differentiate on the basis of PXRD alone, Ni K-edge extended
X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) analysis of a sample of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ revealed a spectrum that better agrees with a simulated spectrum of AB than of AA. As shown in Figure S3, a strong $\mathrm{{Ni\cdotsNi}}$ scattering path at $R\ =\ 3.0$ Å (actual $\mathrm{Ni\cdotsNi}$ distance $=\ 3.33\ \mathrm{~\AA})$ , expected for AA, is absent in the experimental data.
Additional support for a slipped-parallel AB stacking model came from DFT calculations (see SI for full details). A model structure was developed by optimizing a hydrogen-terminated fragment at the B3LYP-D3BJ/ ${\mathrm{'}}6{\cdot}31{\mathrm{G}}^{*}$ level and constraining it to P6/mmm symmetry. A unit cell for the structure was then made by fixing an interlayer separation of 3.33 Å. Periodic single-point energy calculations using the GGA-PBE exchangecorrelation functional with D2 dispersion correction were carried out for structures with 82 different $a b$ -plane displacements. The potential energy surface (PES) shown in Figure 2 was obtained by interpolating DFT total energies using 2D
Lagrange polynomials. This PES suggested that the fully eclipsed AA structure is energetically unfavorable and that the AB sequence is the most stable. In particular, an AB model wherein the 2D unit cell of one layer is slipped relative to a neighboring layer by slightly more than $1/16\mathrm{th}$ of a cell edge $(\sim\mathrm{\tilde{1.8}\AA})$ along the $a$ or $b$ vectors gave the lowest energy on the PES. These calculations do not rule out closely related slippedparallel structures containing a mixture of displacements to these minima in the PES, which is consistent with the poorer long-range order along the c direction. Altogether, the PXRD, EXAFS, and DFT data evidence a hexagonal $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ structure with slipped-parallel stacking and unit cell parameters $a=b=21.75\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{\dot{c}=6.66\mathrm{~\AA~}}\end{array}$
With a good structural model in hand, we turned our attention to studying the electronic properties of both bulk and thin films of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . To allow for optical measurements, we chose quartz as a substrate for our films. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) of representative films of ${\sim}500~\mathrm{nm}$ thickness, shown in Figure 3, evidence macroporous surface features and excellent film coverage on the quartz substrate. Importantly, the electronic absorption features of such films extend well into the nearinfrared (NIR) range $\mathit{\lambda}_{\mathrm{\lambda}_{\mathrm{max}}}^{\mathrm{~'~}}=1400\ \mathrm{nm},$ , as shown in the UV− vis−NIR spectrum in Figure S4. Such low-energy electronic excitations are common in highly conjugated organics and conducting polymers34 but are absent in molecular complexes such as $\mathrm{Ni}\bar{(\mathrm{i}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{q})_{2}},$ which do not absorb at wavelengths above 900 nm.29,33
Encouraged by the possibility of extended 2D conjugation, we measured the electrical properties of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . To avoid swelling and other solvent effects, we set out to dry our samples for these measurements. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of bulk, freshly cleaned $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},$ shown in Figure S5, suggested that heating at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ would eliminate all water, and samples used for electrical measurements were dried accordingly.
The first indication of excellent electrical properties in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ came from a two-probe measurement of bulk powder compressed as a pellet between two stainless steel rods.35 Despite the small particle size, the numerous inter-grain boundaries, and the electrode contact resistances, the pellet conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was $2~{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . This is on the same order of magnitude as the two-probe pellet conductivity of some of the best organic conductors,35 including the iconic TTF-TCNQ $(\sigma_{\mathrm{{pellet}}}~\doteq~10~\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ .36 Furthermore, the bulk conductivity of $\hat{\mathrm{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is 1 order of magnitude higher than that of the best $s{\mathrm{.}}$ -MOG (Ni-hexathiobenzene, $\sigma=0.15\mathrm{{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}},$ ), and at least 2 orders of magnitude better than those of the most conducting MOFs to date.37−39
Even more remarkable results were obtained for thin films, configurations that are likely more relevant for future devices. Thus, films of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ deposited on quartz substrates consistently gave conductivity values of $40\ \mathrm{\bar{S}}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature, as measured by the van der Pauw method.40 The conductivity showed a linear increase with temperature from 77 to $450~\mathrm{K},$ as shown in Figure 4, a behavior that was reversible upon cooling. Although we have been unable to find a model that unequivocally explains this behavior over the entire temperature range, organic-based semiconductors are notorious for having complicated, mixed charge transport mechanisms; $\scriptstyle41-43$ further theoretical and experimental studies will probe the mechanism that is operative here. Most notably, among all coordination polymers,44 the film conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ is exceeded only by that of $\mathtt{C u}$ (4-hydroxythiophenolate) (σ = 120 S·cm−1).45
Clearly, the nature of charge transport in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and its potentially superior single sheet electrical properties warrant further studies, as do its magnetic behavior and its inclusion in electronic devices. The more general picture afforded by these studies, nevertheless, is that metals can mediate very efficient 2D conjugation pathways between electroactive organic molecules, leading to new materials with impressive electrical properties. Although this could perhaps be already gleaned from the vast literature on molecular conductors, the successful application of these concepts to two- and three-dimensionally extended crystalline networks is just emerging and promises to take us on a thrilling intellectual ride.
